00:20:45.213 --> 00:20:47.050
and she made me clean out the garage

535
00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:48.320
and I found my college essay.

536
00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:49.153
And I thought,

537
00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.540
it's very strange to be looking back at this person

538
00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:58.390
and understanding what stayed the same.

539
00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:00.250
Many things stayed the same, you know.

540
00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:04.870
Essentially this relationship I have with the water

541
00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:06.740
and also with writing and creativity,

542
00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:09.020
that's something that I trace back to my dad.

543
00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:09.870
Like very much so.
Yeah.

544
00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:11.840
He was the permissive free spirit.

545
00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:13.510
Yeah, absolutely.

546
00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:16.860
You know, he was fun and he was a kid

547
00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:21.130
and he's today, at 73 years old, he's still a kid.

548
00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:23.440
He actually hasn't changed that much

549
00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:24.730
but I have, you know.

550
00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:27.110
Was your mom more of the task master then?

551
00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:28.220
Oh, totally.

552
00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:29.540
She was the strict one.

553
00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:31.380
I don't actually remember her smiling.

554
00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:32.540
It's terrible.

555
00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:33.860
It's so terrible.

556
00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:36.380
But, you know, after they divorced

557
00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.218
which was really, really painful,

558
00:21:38.218 --> 00:21:42.103
they didn't finalize their divorce until I was in college.

559
00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:47.060
But then I got to know my mom as this, as who she was.

560
00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:50.990
She was a person who was not in relationship to my dad.

561
00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:52.100
She was fun.

562
00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:53.683
She had her own ideas.

563
00:21:56.502 --> 00:21:57.702
She had stories to tell.

564
00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:02.850
That's when I got to know her, you know, as a real person.

565
00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:04.807
You need both of those.

566
00:22:04.807 --> 00:22:05.640
You know?

567
00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.470
I think you need the artistic sensibility

568
00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:11.130
but you need the regimented person as well.

569
00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:12.574
Oh yeah.

570
00:22:12.574 --> 00:22:15.457
You know, she was the one who said, you know,

571
00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:22.050
my desire for order, my desire to prepare,

572
00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:24.030
like all of those things come from her.

573
00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:29.030
Like my sort of risk averse (laughing)

574
00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:32.020
older person self is because of her.

575
00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:33.980
And I say those things lovingly.

576
00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:37.140
And because I understand that those are things that

577
00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.610
you need to operate

578
00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:42.200
and be a responsible human in this world.

579
00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:45.330
And I take the fun and the creativity

580
00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.130
and the light from my dad.

581
00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:50.700
But I also know that in the context of our family,

582
00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:52.540
that really ruined our family,

583
00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:55.170
this sort of shirking of responsibility

584
00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:58.900
and not owning up to that.

585
00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:02.730
So profoundly both parents

586
00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:05.090
have shaped me to be who I am.

587
00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:06.330
Yeah, you came out pretty good.

588
00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:07.163
Oh, thanks!

589
00:23:07.163 --> 00:23:08.490
So it worked out, you know.

590
00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:09.323
It could have gone

591
00:23:09.323 --> 00:23:10.993
terribly wrong.
You go to Harvard, right?

592
00:23:11.892 --> 00:23:13.360
And yet you're an artist.

593
00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:15.410
So you have, you know how to focus

594
00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:17.260
and, you know, organize your life

595
00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:20.160
but you can also be a free spirit and creative

596
00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.440
and you know, follow these whimsical

597
00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:26.150
passions that you have to track down these stories.

598
00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:26.983
Yeah.

599
00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:33.640
My mom, I remember my mom said to a good friend of mine

600
00:23:37.191 --> 00:23:40.280
this was when I was 22.

601
00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:43.560
and I took off for a few months to go

602
00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:45.740
on a fellowship to New Zealand.

603
00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.930
And I was, came back and I had student loans to pay off.

604
00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:50.810
And I said, I really want to work

605
00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:52.460
at this adventure magazine.

606
00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:53.780
You know, they're not paying me right now

607
00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:55.770
but I just, I'll just you know,

608
00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:58.340
take a waitressing job or something in New York

609
00:23:58.340 --> 00:23:59.173
while I'm doing it.

610
00:23:59.173 --> 00:24:01.900
And she said, she turned to my friend and she said

611
00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:03.460
Bonnie is such a free spirit.

612
00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:05.080
And she, it was not a compliment.

613
00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:05.913
Right.

614
00:24:09.443 --> 00:24:12.410
But you know what, though, to her credit

615
00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:14.410
like a couple of weeks later, she said,

616
00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:16.563
you know if you really want to do this,

617
00:24:17.420 --> 00:24:19.400
I will support you in this.

618
00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:22.270
And I did, I did it for six months.

619
00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:26.250
And then I got a job that paid money, actual money.

620
00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:28.100
And there we go.

621
00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:29.790
That was probably a big step for her.

622
00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:31.090
It was.

623
00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:32.630
Actually, now that I think about it,

624
00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:33.463
it really was.

625
00:24:33.463 --> 00:24:36.015
I have to ask her about it if she remembers.

626
00:24:36.015 --> 00:24:39.370
But you grew up in and around pools all the time,

627
00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:41.503
swam competitively, you and your brother.

628
00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:44.790
But you didn't end up swimming in college, right.

629
00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:46.350
You rode crew in college.
No.

630
00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:48.580
Yeah, by the time I got to college

631
00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:50.510
and I wasn't good enough to swim in college,

632
00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:52.033
like not, no.

633
00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:56.950
But I was done with competitive swimming

634
00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:59.690
because college, to me represented like something new,

635
00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:01.600
like something super fun and exciting.

636
00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:04.563
And I always knew that I was going to,

637
00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:08.380
college was going to be in the place where I got

638
00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:10.590
to meet new people and do new things.

639
00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:15.590
And I very much felt that I wanted to do that.

640
00:25:15.672 --> 00:25:17.060
Cause I wasn't, you know,

641
00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:20.313
I think I was a very old person when I was like a kid.

642
00:25:21.861 --> 00:25:25.290
You know, I was like, I want to be 20.

643
00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.421
And I am eight, (laughing) something like that.

644
00:25:28.421 --> 00:25:29.254
I get it.

645
00:25:29.254 --> 00:25:31.463
I just wanted to have autonomy, I think.

646
00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:35.253
And I rode crew.

647
00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:38.210
You know it's so quintessential

648
00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:40.933
it's almost a cliche to be like rowing.

649
00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:42.983
Sculling in the

650
00:25:42.983 --> 00:25:44.997
Charles river.
At an Ivy League college.

651
00:25:44.997 --> 00:25:46.480
Yeah.

652
00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:47.790
It's like the Ivy is like, you know.

653
00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:49.680
Within sight as you're on the water.

654
00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:51.110
Still a water sport though.

655
00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:52.530
It is, I wanted,

656
00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:55.250
and I was talking to a friend about this recently.

657
00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:59.970
I just thought it was so cool

658
00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:01.700
to have a different perspective of water.

659
00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:03.980
And when, you know, when you're swimming

660
00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:05.390
it's so solo, right?

661
00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:07.940
You're on a team, but the experience of swimming

662
00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:11.892
is very much you in the water.

663
00:26:11.892 --> 00:26:13.570
And when you're in a boat with, you know

664
00:26:13.570 --> 00:26:14.810
seven other people

665
00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:17.320
and you have to become one,

666
00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:22.140
you have to move in unison almost all of the time.

667
00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:24.010
I would say like 98% of the time,

668
00:26:24.010 --> 00:26:26.700
you can feel like the slight tug of someone else

669
00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:29.113
who's not quite in your sync.

670
00:26:30.013 --> 00:26:33.640
And then the 2% of the time when you are flying together

671
00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:35.020
it's just so magical.

672
00:26:35.020 --> 00:26:39.280
It's like, it's as if you are taking flight

673
00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:41.517
and you didn't even, you're not trying.

674
00:26:42.612 --> 00:26:45.340
And I was thinking this the other day, because I wanted

675
00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:47.935
I really loved experiencing water

676
00:26:47.935 --> 00:26:50.700
from a different perspective.

677
00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:54.350
And then I only quit because

678
00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:59.614
you know, you're looking at me and I'm not a giant person.

679
00:26:59.614 --> 00:27:01.570
You know, I'm probably between like a coxswain size

680
00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:03.730
and a lightweight river size.

681
00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:04.914
You're not small enough to be a coxswain.

682
00:27:04.914 --> 00:27:09.450
I was not, I'm only 5'4.

683
00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:10.373
I was like,

684
00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:16.840
the coach said to me, "Your technique is great.

685
00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:20.180
I put you in stroke in this boat, you know

686
00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:23.140
but you have to put on like 20 pounds."

687
00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:24.770
And I had already packed on, like

688
00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.210
I was eating like a crazy person, you know

689
00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:29.100
in the freshman dining hall.

690
00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:30.530
I was working out in the weight room,

691
00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:31.970
I was erging like a maniac.

692
00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:35.560
And I just was like, I think I have maxed out.

693
00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:38.373
I don't know that I can gain any more weight to do this.

694
00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:40.670
It was weird.

695
00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:41.740
So you were like, I'm done?

696
00:27:41.740 --> 00:27:43.320
Well then I joined water polo.

697
00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:44.793
Oh, you did? Okay.

698
00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:46.920
Again, another water sport.

699
00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:47.753
Yeah.

700
00:27:47.753 --> 00:27:48.586
But it was fun.

701
00:27:48.586 --> 00:27:49.419
I mean, it was hard.

702
00:27:49.419 --> 00:27:51.650
I just realized that after awhile that

703
00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:55.320
as playing a sport in college

704
00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:57.440
and you know this more than anyone is,

705
00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:58.350
that's all you're doing.

706
00:27:58.350 --> 00:27:59.183
Yeah.

707
00:27:59.183 --> 00:28:00.760
It's pretty all encompassing.

708
00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.110
And I didn't want that.

709
00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.180
You wanted to broaden your

710
00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:05.985
aperture, your horizons.
Yeah, yeah.

711
00:28:05.985 --> 00:28:07.448
Totally.
Yeah, I get it.

712
00:28:07.448 --> 00:28:11.010
What's interesting about your relationship

713
00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:13.760
to swimming in high school

714
00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:15.990
is that the pool represented

715
00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:17.920
like this refuge for you, right?

716
00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:20.530
Like you had a complicated relationship

717
00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:23.180
with your peers in school, but at the pool,

718
00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:25.690
which was a much more diverse

719
00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:27.340
you know, it was literally a pool

720
00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:29.540
of all different kinds of people.

721
00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:31.440
It was kind of like a refuge,

722
00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:33.980
like a place where you felt at home.

723
00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:36.870
And I think that's worth exploring a little bit.

724
00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:39.080
And I really liked how you did

725
00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:42.080
this kind of archeological dig into the history

726
00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:44.690
of the United States

727
00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:46.280
and its relationship with swimming

728
00:28:46.280 --> 00:28:48.820
and in particular you know, pool building

729
00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:52.660
and access to pools and how that changed over time.

730
00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:53.870
So let's talk about that a little bit.

731
00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:56.330
Cause I think it's pretty interesting.

732
00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:58.463
I know, like I grew up in the Northeast.

733
00:28:59.390 --> 00:29:04.270
And you know, most high schools don't have pools

734
00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:08.350
whereas in other parts of the country they do.

735
00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.180
And that tracks back to our, you know,

736
00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:13.903
sort of checkered relationship with race and segregation.

737
00:29:17.703 --> 00:29:19.920
I really did not appreciate,

738
00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:20.990
meaning I didn't understand

739
00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:24.810
the extent to which

740
00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:31.410
the current, you know, gap, the racial gap

741
00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:36.290
in swimming ability between especially blacks and whites

742
00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:39.330
in this country is so traced back

743
00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:44.330
to this era of segregation in our country.

744
00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:49.590
And the fact that it persists to the degree that it does,

745
00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.680
I mean, I think the rate is that

746
00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:53.170
the latest statistics show

747
00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:56.660
that black kids are five times.

748
00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.830
They drown at a rate five times that of white children.

749
00:29:58.830 --> 00:29:59.663
Right.

750
00:29:59.663 --> 00:30:03.743
I mean, that is just , it's horrible.

751
00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:11.700
And then it's not just swimming access and education,

752
00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.740
but it's also then when you break through to join a team

753
00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:17.000
you find a team you're swimming, you love it.

754
00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:20.195
And then you feel like you're the only one.

755
00:30:20.195 --> 00:30:23.283
You know, swimming remains a very white sport.

756
00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:26.430
And you don't feel welcome

757
00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:29.940
or you don't feel that you belong

758
00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:34.830
or that you have positive images and stories.

759
00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:39.890
I think that that has a really profound effect

760
00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:42.130
on whether you want to keep doing something, you know?

761
00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:45.740
So like I recently got to talk to

762
00:30:47.060 --> 00:30:49.130
the founders of an organization called Black Can Swim.

763
00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:52.490
And Ebony Roseman is the founder.

764
00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:54.291
And she was talking about how

765
00:30:54.291 --> 00:30:57.660
when her daughter started swimming,

766
00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:58.880
and then I think the reason

767
00:30:58.880 --> 00:31:00.210
that they started the organization

768
00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:03.310
was because when they Googled like black kids swim,

769
00:31:03.310 --> 00:31:05.573
they would come up with,

770
00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.120
the top Google hits were like

771
00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:10.930
black people can't swim or black kids

772
00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:11.763
don't know how to swim.

773
00:31:11.763 --> 00:31:13.410
Well, when I was growing up as a kid

774
00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:15.610
there was this trope like,

775
00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:17.070
oh, well, you know black kids can't swim

776
00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:20.490
or they have a higher body density or something like that.

777
00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:22.460
Like some crazy reason why

778
00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:24.630
black people weren't good at swimming.

779
00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:26.770
Which is just ridiculous.

780
00:31:26.770 --> 00:31:29.794
And then that is pervasive and it persists.

781
00:31:29.794 --> 00:31:31.970
And those are the ridiculous things

782
00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:34.170
that I heard growing up too.

783
00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:36.010
At the same time,

784
00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:39.490
I think of my experience, you know,

785
00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:42.500
as you pointed to

786
00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.587
was really special for me, because I grew up,

787
00:31:45.587 --> 00:31:48.690
I swam on a team that was in the next town over

788
00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:50.920
and it was a very diverse community.

789
00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:53.140
Freeport, New York is, you know

790
00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:57.860
has a large African-American and Latino demographic.

791
00:31:57.860 --> 00:32:00.380
And it also, the team also attracted kids

792
00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:02.760
from all across Long Island.

793
00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:06.470
And, you know, even though I was one of the,

794
00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:08.840
you know "the onlys" in my high school,

795
00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:10.473
a town away two towns away,

796
00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:13.520
that pool was super diverse.

797
00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:18.520
You know, the head guard, at that pool where I lifeguarded

798
00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:22.080
at Freeport Rec was, was black, you know?

799
00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:24.150
