to kind of reintroduce to the world

2357
01:37:10.190 --> 01:37:12.060
and perhaps introduce for the first time

2358
01:37:12.060 --> 01:37:14.390
what the sort of foundation of

2359
01:37:16.490 --> 01:37:20.110
Japanese swimming, you know,

2360
01:37:20.110 --> 01:37:22.640
the national team success that we know it today.

2361
01:37:22.640 --> 01:37:24.190
So much of it breaststroke, right?

2362
01:37:24.190 --> 01:37:26.580
So they've been, Japanese members have been really dominant

2363
01:37:26.580 --> 01:37:27.670
in breaststroke for a long time.

2364
01:37:27.670 --> 01:37:30.630
And there's an interesting historical link

2365
01:37:30.630 --> 01:37:33.073
to the techniques of Nihon Eiho.

2366
01:37:34.030 --> 01:37:35.940
I mean, I remember talking to

2367
01:37:37.140 --> 01:37:39.980
one of these masters of the art

2368
01:37:39.980 --> 01:37:41.837
and he was trying to explain it.

2369
01:37:41.837 --> 01:37:46.300
And we were observing a class in Yokohama

2370
01:37:46.300 --> 01:37:50.430
of these swimmers taking the class.

2371
01:37:50.430 --> 01:37:52.740
And he said, you know,

2372
01:37:52.740 --> 01:37:54.180
we were observing the glide

2373
01:37:54.180 --> 01:37:56.660
and the certain formations that they would be swimming in.

2374
01:37:56.660 --> 01:37:58.630
And he said, you know, Kitajima,

2375
01:37:58.630 --> 01:38:00.730
you know Kosuke Kitajima, which is like,

2376
01:38:01.816 --> 01:38:03.300
The world record holder, 100 meter breastroke, right?

2377
01:38:03.300 --> 01:38:06.860
Just this dominant breaststroker

2378
01:38:06.860 --> 01:38:10.600
known for like this incredible glide.

2379
01:38:10.600 --> 01:38:12.430
And he said, that's Nihon Eiho.

2380
01:38:12.430 --> 01:38:15.980
Like, he was just like, that's the skill that we teach.

2381
01:38:15.980 --> 01:38:18.803
And that has been ingrained into like coaching.

2382
01:38:19.860 --> 01:38:20.693
Yeah.

2383
01:38:21.684 --> 01:38:26.560
it's fascinating to track that,

2384
01:38:26.560 --> 01:38:29.770
you know the antecedent of Japanese swimming success

2385
01:38:29.770 --> 01:38:30.710
back to that practice.

2386
01:38:30.710 --> 01:38:34.860
And I didn't know that the Japanese were so dominant

2387
01:38:34.860 --> 01:38:36.080
at the end of the 1920s,

2388
01:38:36.080 --> 01:38:38.760
like the 1934 Olympics they won like 12 medals

2389
01:38:38.760 --> 01:38:39.593
or something like that.

2390
01:38:39.593 --> 01:38:40.426
I had no idea that,

2391
01:38:40.426 --> 01:38:41.259
They like blasted

2392
01:38:41.259 --> 01:38:42.821
onto the scene.
They were crushing it

2393
01:38:42.821 --> 01:38:43.820
back then.

2394
01:38:43.820 --> 01:38:44.780
That was crazy.

2395
01:38:44.780 --> 01:38:48.140
And it's all rooted in this practice

2396
01:38:48.140 --> 01:38:49.800
which my sense is

2397
01:38:51.330 --> 01:38:53.430
it's not quite a Tai Chi thing

2398
01:38:53.430 --> 01:38:57.850
but maybe where they might share some sensibility

2399
01:38:57.850 --> 01:38:59.150
is this idea of

2400
01:39:00.110 --> 01:39:01.520
learning how to use,

2401
01:39:01.520 --> 01:39:03.220
learning how to work with the water,

2402
01:39:03.220 --> 01:39:05.000
like being symbiotic with the water

2403
01:39:05.000 --> 01:39:06.440
and using it to your advantage.

2404
01:39:06.440 --> 01:39:10.180
Like, we all know the swimmer who isn't so experienced

2405
01:39:10.180 --> 01:39:11.920
and they're in a race

2406
01:39:11.920 --> 01:39:13.865
or in a triathlon or something like that.

2407
01:39:13.865 --> 01:39:15.100
And they're fighting the water.

2408
01:39:15.100 --> 01:39:15.950
They're fighting, it's obvious.

2409
01:39:15.950 --> 01:39:17.660
They're making the water work against them.

2410
01:39:17.660 --> 01:39:18.970
You know what I mean?

2411
01:39:18.970 --> 01:39:22.340
And really, you know swimming is about fitness of course,

2412
01:39:22.340 --> 01:39:24.650
and technique but it's really about finesse.

2413
01:39:24.650 --> 01:39:29.650
It's this delicate relationship

2414
01:39:29.740 --> 01:39:31.130
and this touch and this feel

2415
01:39:31.130 --> 01:39:32.117
that you develop over time

2416
01:39:32.117 --> 01:39:34.670
and some have an innate talent for

2417
01:39:34.670 --> 01:39:37.570
where they just know how to flow with the water

2418
01:39:37.570 --> 01:39:38.610
and make it work for them.

2419
01:39:38.610 --> 01:39:41.000
And those tend to be the most successful swimmers.

2420
01:39:41.000 --> 01:39:43.830
I think that the Tai Chi analogy

2421
01:39:43.830 --> 01:39:46.400
is really salient in this context.

2422
01:39:46.400 --> 01:39:51.030
I mean, it is about working with the element

2423
01:39:51.030 --> 01:39:53.860
and figuring out how to

2424
01:39:55.080 --> 01:39:56.950
use it to your advantage

2425
01:39:56.950 --> 01:39:58.970
and be really efficient too.

2426
01:39:58.970 --> 01:40:00.520
I think that's something that

2427
01:40:01.820 --> 01:40:04.100
people don't really think about with swimming.

2428
01:40:04.100 --> 01:40:06.770
And they think that it's like you said,

2429
01:40:06.770 --> 01:40:09.780
when people are starting to swim

2430
01:40:09.780 --> 01:40:13.350
or starting to learn how to swim competitively and go fast,

2431
01:40:13.350 --> 01:40:15.000
like there's so much muscling,

2432
01:40:15.000 --> 01:40:16.820
there's so much over,

2433
01:40:16.820 --> 01:40:17.980
like, windmilling, you know.

2434
01:40:17.980 --> 01:40:21.650
You're just trying to like overpower it with brute force.

2435
01:40:21.650 --> 01:40:23.750
And really it's about like,

2436
01:40:23.750 --> 01:40:26.113
your timing, your angle of entry,

2437
01:40:26.950 --> 01:40:28.800
the position of your body in the water.

2438
01:40:28.800 --> 01:40:31.200
And if you have that, it's actually quite,

2439
01:40:31.200 --> 01:40:33.770
you're not expending a lot of energy.

2440
01:40:33.770 --> 01:40:35.930
One of the things that's interesting about Lynn Cox

2441
01:40:35.930 --> 01:40:38.280
is that her buoyancy is such that

2442
01:40:39.130 --> 01:40:41.780
in salt water, she's like neutrally buoyant.

2443
01:40:41.780 --> 01:40:43.310
So she's in that perfect

2444
01:40:44.490 --> 01:40:46.830
body position to be swimming

2445
01:40:46.830 --> 01:40:49.190
for hours and hours and hours

2446
01:40:49.190 --> 01:40:50.170
and miles and miles and miles

2447
01:40:50.170 --> 01:40:51.350
because she has that.

2448
01:40:51.350 --> 01:40:53.120
She's riding a little bit higher

2449
01:40:53.120 --> 01:40:54.310
than your average person would.

2450
01:40:54.310 --> 01:40:56.310
You know, and she's talked about that.

2451
01:40:58.120 --> 01:40:59.720
So that also helps her, you know

2452
01:40:59.720 --> 01:41:02.023
with her very, very long swims.

2453
01:41:03.070 --> 01:41:05.920
But it's also about touch and finesse, like you said.

2454
01:41:05.920 --> 01:41:07.410
For sure.

2455
01:41:07.410 --> 01:41:09.760
And in this practice, they have much like karate

2456
01:41:09.760 --> 01:41:12.415
like you get lines on your cap or whatever.

2457
01:41:12.415 --> 01:41:13.296
Yeah.

2458
01:41:13.296 --> 01:41:14.600
Each like level that you achieve.

2459
01:41:14.600 --> 01:41:16.800
I think it was like stripes in some cases.

2460
01:41:18.000 --> 01:41:20.580
Cause I asked them, I said how do you know who's the master?

2461
01:41:20.580 --> 01:41:21.670
And they said, look at the caps.

2462
01:41:21.670 --> 01:41:23.570
And they all have their special caps.

2463
01:41:23.570 --> 01:41:26.203
And it was so cool to learn about it.

2464
01:41:27.250 --> 01:41:30.803
And I hope to get the opportunity,

2465
01:41:30.803 --> 01:41:34.070
I actually had been trying and planning to get

2466
01:41:34.070 --> 01:41:39.070
to the Tokyo Olympics so I could see that, you know, happen.

2467
01:41:39.320 --> 01:41:40.910
See that sort of re-introduction

2468
01:41:40.910 --> 01:41:44.120
of Nihon Eiho to the world.

2469
01:41:44.120 --> 01:41:45.030
What's your sense of

2470
01:41:45.030 --> 01:41:47.020
whether the Olympics are going to happen or not?

2471
01:41:47.020 --> 01:41:48.230
Oh my gosh, well,

2472
01:41:48.230 --> 01:41:49.480
can they make a bubble big enough?

2473
01:41:49.480 --> 01:41:51.030
I don't know.

2474
01:41:51.030 --> 01:41:51.993
Right, what do you think?

2475
01:41:51.993 --> 01:41:53.883
I don't know, I don't know.

2476
01:41:55.550 --> 01:41:56.383
I don't know.

2477
01:41:56.383 --> 01:41:57.216
It's changing so quickly.

2478
01:41:57.216 --> 01:41:59.010
I mean, the spikes that we're seeing right now

2479
01:41:59.010 --> 01:42:01.170
make me feel pessimistic about it,

2480
01:42:01.170 --> 01:42:02.400
but we'll have to see.

2481
01:42:02.400 --> 01:42:03.940
But at some point they have to make a decision.

2482
01:42:03.940 --> 01:42:06.520
There's so many moving pieces and so much money at stake.

2483
01:42:06.520 --> 01:42:08.910
They can't just snap their fingers right beforehand

2484
01:42:08.910 --> 01:42:10.500
and say it's happening or it's not.

2485
01:42:10.500 --> 01:42:13.770
Like at what point do you pass that point of no return

2486
01:42:13.770 --> 01:42:15.750
in terms of the green light or the red light?

2487
01:42:15.750 --> 01:42:16.870
Well, now we're getting,

2488
01:42:16.870 --> 01:42:18.770
and then we'll be getting to the point where then

2489
01:42:18.770 --> 01:42:22.377
it isn't the same year as the winter Olympic year, right?

2490
01:42:22.377 --> 01:42:24.530
Again, which is kind of ironic.

2491
01:42:24.530 --> 01:42:25.851
I know.

2492
01:42:25.851 --> 01:42:27.497
Can you imagine being an Olympic athlete

2493
01:42:27.497 --> 01:42:30.640
and living in that space of not knowing?

2494
01:42:30.640 --> 01:42:34.290
Yeah, it's like a state of suspended animation

2495
01:42:34.290 --> 01:42:35.883
and I think it's so,

2496
01:42:37.030 --> 01:42:39.580
I so feel for those athletes who are

2497
01:42:40.500 --> 01:42:41.650
not knowing, you know they're training

2498
01:42:41.650 --> 01:42:43.630
and then they have had to calibrate their training

2499
01:42:43.630 --> 01:42:45.500
for another year.

2500
01:42:45.500 --> 01:42:47.915
And then I think it will like break a lot of people

2501
01:42:47.915 --> 01:42:48.963
if it doesn't happen.

2502
01:42:48.963 --> 01:42:50.350
I mean it'll play to the advantage

2503
01:42:50.350 --> 01:42:53.300
of the younger less experienced athlete, I suppose

2504
01:42:53.300 --> 01:42:55.970
who benefits with additional time

2505
01:42:55.970 --> 01:42:58.240
but there's so many people that are hanging on

2506
01:42:58.240 --> 01:43:00.920
and trying to make ends meet while they do it.

2507
01:43:00.920 --> 01:43:03.780
And without that certainty

2508
01:43:03.780 --> 01:43:07.531
it's probably really difficult to get sponsor support.

2509
01:43:07.531 --> 01:43:09.220
Like it's just gotta be really challenging.

2510
01:43:09.220 --> 01:43:12.060
It turns out it's expensive to be an athlete.

2511
01:43:12.060 --> 01:43:14.490
It is, right?

2512
01:43:14.490 --> 01:43:15.763
It's expensive to live.

2513
01:43:17.030 --> 01:43:18.840
Another idea that I really love about the book

2514
01:43:18.840 --> 01:43:21.240
is this idea that to be a swimmer

2515
01:43:21.240 --> 01:43:25.470
is to be a seeker on some level.

2516
01:43:25.470 --> 01:43:28.120
And when you track through history

2517
01:43:28.120 --> 01:43:31.340
there are so many leaders and great thinkers

2518
01:43:31.340 --> 01:43:34.550
who had this profound relationship with the water.

2519
01:43:34.550 --> 01:43:36.990
From the row to Lord Byron

2520
01:43:36.990 --> 01:43:38.220
and, you know, even JFK,

2521
01:43:38.220 --> 01:43:42.840
like people who would gravitate towards the water

2522
01:43:42.840 --> 01:43:46.250
in times of crisis or as a daily practice

2523
01:43:46.250 --> 01:43:50.420
to basically help them be more self-actualized,

2524
01:43:50.420 --> 01:43:51.510
better human beings.

2525
01:43:51.510 --> 01:43:52.820
Right.

2526
01:43:52.820 --> 01:43:55.997
I love that FDR was the one who put the pool in.

2527
01:43:55.997 --> 01:43:59.230
You know, FDR put the pool in the white house

2528
01:43:59.230 --> 01:44:03.870
and it lives under the white house press pool briefing room

2529
01:44:03.870 --> 01:44:05.640
which is just,

2530
01:44:05.640 --> 01:44:07.830
Is that why it's called the press pool?

2531
01:44:07.830 --> 01:44:08.950
I don't actually know.

2532
01:44:08.950 --> 01:44:13.520
I've never quite been able to pinpoint if that's why.

2533
01:44:13.520 --> 01:44:16.053
But I recently had,

2534
01:44:17.390 --> 01:44:20.860
yeah, I recently was talking about this with someone

2535
01:44:20.860 --> 01:44:23.220
and you know, one of the reporters at NPR

2536
01:44:23.220 --> 01:44:26.570
who reports in the white house said,

2537
01:44:26.570 --> 01:44:29.330
yes, you can see the tile.

2538
01:44:29.330 --> 01:44:30.440
So you'd be in the room.

2539
01:44:30.440 --> 01:44:32.200
So it's underneath the room,

2540
01:44:32.200 --> 01:44:34.390
but you can still see the tile

2541
01:44:34.390 --> 01:44:37.670
and like where the pool actually

2542
01:44:37.670 --> 01:44:42.380
is a bunch of like internet servers and stuff. (laughing)

2543
01:44:42.380 --> 01:44:43.880
And I thought that is,

2544
01:44:43.880 --> 01:44:46.250
oh, and that Hillary Clinton wanted

2545
01:44:46.250 --> 01:44:47.320
when they moved into the white house,

2546
01:44:47.320 --> 01:44:51.030
she wanted to reopen the pool again as a pool.

2547
01:44:51.030 --> 01:44:52.340
Somehow that didn't happen.

2548
01:44:52.340 --> 01:44:56.455
But I thought, wow, that would have been great.

2549
01:44:56.455 --> 01:44:57.288
Right.

2550
01:44:57.288 --> 01:45:00.730
Well, I know JFK swam a lot.

2551
01:45:00.730 --> 01:45:03.080
And in particular it was very helpful

2552
01:45:03.080 --> 01:45:05.850
when he was in the middle of the Cuban missile crisis

2553
01:45:05.850 --> 01:45:09.700
to try to like get some distance and balance.

2554
01:45:09.700 --> 01:45:12.030
So he could solve this unsolvable problems.

2555
01:45:12.030 --> 01:45:13.710
Isn't it fun to think about swimming

2556
01:45:13.710 --> 01:45:18.530
as like a a hidden actor in these moments of history, maybe?

2557
01:45:18.530 --> 01:45:19.440
That's a good one.

2558
01:45:19.440 --> 01:45:22.540
Well and imagine being FDR, the freedom, you know

2559
01:45:22.540 --> 01:45:24.180
of being able to get out of the wheelchair

2560
01:45:24.180 --> 01:45:25.393
and move your body.

2561
01:45:25.393 --> 01:45:26.380
Well and that he hid his,

2562
01:45:26.380 --> 01:45:27.530
Yeah, nobody knew.

2563
01:45:27.530 --> 01:45:31.043
Yeah, he hid his, it was so hard.

2564
01:45:33.050 --> 01:45:34.772
And then it finally became, you know

2565
01:45:34.772 --> 01:45:36.600
impossible to hide

2566
01:45:36.600 --> 01:45:41.120
his physical, you know, limitations.

2567
01:45:41.120 --> 01:45:41.953
Right.

2568
01:45:41.953 --> 01:45:44.210
So how do you think about that relationship

2569
01:45:44.210 --> 01:45:47.853
between being a seeker and being in the water?

2570
01:45:50.500 --> 01:45:53.723
I mean, I certainly think it's true for myself.

2571
01:45:57.780 --> 01:46:01.220
I wrote my first book, American Chinatown

2572
01:46:01.220 --> 01:46:05.034
about how I noticed that when I would go to a city,

2573
01:46:05.034 --> 01:46:07.650
the first thing I do, I look to see

2574
01:46:07.650 --> 01:46:09.830
if they had a Chinatown and I would go

2575
01:46:09.830 --> 01:46:11.620
and kind of see, it was just interesting.

2576
01:46:11.620 --> 01:46:14.640
It was just a little window to see

2577
01:46:14.640 --> 01:46:18.890
how is this place different or the same to me

2578
01:46:18.890 --> 01:46:21.270
across the Chinese diaspora.

2579
01:46:21.270 --> 01:46:24.590
And then I realized what I do with swimming

2580
01:46:24.590 --> 01:46:25.890
is that I look for a place

2581
01:46:25.890 --> 01:46:27.063
to swim.
The pool, yeah.

2582
01:46:27.063 --> 01:46:28.933
A pool or a body of water.

2583
01:46:30.376 --> 01:46:33.100
How often is the pool in Chinatown?

2584
01:46:33.100 --> 01:46:36.240
Oh, well there's the Chinatown YMCA

2585
01:46:36.240 --> 01:46:38.090
in San Francisco, there you go.

2586
01:46:38.090 --> 01:46:39.560
Gorgeous pool.

2587
01:46:39.560 --> 01:46:43.607
But yeah, not that often, (laughing) it turns out.

2588
01:46:45.142 --> 01:46:47.161
But I don't know.

2589
01:46:47.161 --> 01:46:50.550
I think that water, I think water

2590
01:46:50.550 --> 01:46:52.833
and seeking it is something that,

2591
01:46:54.780 --> 01:46:56.570
for me it's about finding freedom.

2592
01:46:56.570 --> 01:46:58.100
It's about finding a new perspective.

2593
01:46:58.100 --> 01:47:00.180
It's about finding a new way of looking at things.

2594
01:47:00.180 --> 01:47:02.633
And I mean, isn't that what being a writer is?

2595
01:47:03.719 --> 01:47:06.600
So I think for me, it's like these two things

2596
01:47:06.600 --> 01:47:09.650
are hand-in-hand for sure

2597
01:47:09.650 --> 01:47:11.453
about how my mind works that way.

2598
01:47:12.940 --> 01:47:15.570
What's the longest you've gone without swimming?

2599
01:47:15.570 --> 01:47:17.913
Oh, that's a great question.

2600
01:47:20.190 --> 01:47:23.780
Even when I tore my ACL (laughing) many, many years ago,

2601
01:47:23.780 --> 01:47:25.570
oh God, I remember this.

2602
01:47:25.570 --> 01:47:26.900
I had that stupid machine

2603
01:47:26.900 --> 01:47:29.180
that like moves your knee at night.

2604
01:47:29.180 --> 01:47:31.170
And my husband made me sleep on the couch

2605
01:47:31.170 --> 01:47:33.970
because I was like, I'm not sleeping next to that thing.

2606
01:47:35.230 --> 01:47:36.953
Oh God, that was like, what?

2607
01:47:37.930 --> 01:47:39.093
15 years ago?

2608
01:47:40.710 --> 01:47:43.182
I wasn't out of the water for that long,

2609
01:47:43.182 --> 01:47:46.870
but it's because it's rehab, it's recovery.

2610
01:47:46.870 --> 01:47:50.670
It's like one of the first like the only things you could do

2611
01:47:50.670 --> 01:47:54.630
when you're, you know recovering from surgery in many cases.

2612
01:47:54.630 --> 01:47:55.463
Right.

2613
01:47:55.463 --> 01:47:58.290
It's interesting that when people get injured on land

2614
01:47:58.290 --> 01:48:01.510
they send them to the pool to repair themselves.

2615
01:48:01.510 --> 01:48:02.580
And then they go back on land.

2616
01:48:02.580 --> 01:48:05.370
And then pound the crap out of their bodies.

2617
01:48:05.370 --> 01:48:07.700
Yeah I had Laird Hamilton here the other day

2618
01:48:07.700 --> 01:48:10.570
and he's got these legendary pool workouts, you know.

2619
01:48:10.570 --> 01:48:14.750
And part of the philosophy behind it is that very thing

2620
01:48:14.750 --> 01:48:17.120
which is when you injure yourself

2621
01:48:17.120 --> 01:48:19.830
and you go to physio

2622
01:48:19.830 --> 01:48:22.310
they teach you all these rudimentary like things

2623
01:48:22.310 --> 01:48:24.570
and you kind of do them for a while and then you're healed

2624
01:48:24.570 --> 01:48:25.610
and then you stopped doing them.

2625
01:48:25.610 --> 01:48:27.790
And he's like, we should just keep doing them.

2626
01:48:27.790 --> 01:48:31.070
And what if we do some of the more rigorous exercises

2627
01:48:31.070 --> 01:48:33.660
in the pool where you remove the thing

2628
01:48:33.660 --> 01:48:35.670
that's making you injured in the first place

2629
01:48:35.670 --> 01:48:37.410
and create the supportive environment

2630
01:48:37.410 --> 01:48:41.040
that allows you to do more with less risk?

2631
01:48:41.040 --> 01:48:43.170
Laird Hamilton is basically saying

2632
01:48:43.170 --> 01:48:46.440
what every physical therapist is like yelling

2633
01:48:46.440 --> 01:48:49.520
at their patient and saying, just keep doing it.

2634
01:48:49.520 --> 01:48:50.780
Why don't you keep doing it?

2635
01:48:50.780 --> 01:48:52.326
My brother's a PT.

2636
01:48:52.326 --> 01:48:54.982
So I hear him lamenting all the time.

2637
01:48:54.982 --> 01:48:58.640
And he says to me, cause I said my elbow and my shoulder

2638
01:48:58.640 --> 01:49:00.847
were sort of bothering me the other day, he said

2639
01:49:00.847 --> 01:49:03.350
"So did you stop doing the exercises that I gave you?"

2640
01:49:03.350 --> 01:49:04.183
And I said "Yes."
Of course I did.

2641
01:49:04.183 --> 01:49:05.630
I could go back and swim.

2642
01:49:05.630 --> 01:49:06.710
So that was in the past.

2643
01:49:06.710 --> 01:49:08.210
So then I stopped of course.

2644
01:49:09.200 --> 01:49:13.000
Let's talk about this guy in Iraq,

2645
01:49:13.000 --> 01:49:16.084
Iraq, who was teaching swimming lessons

2646
01:49:16.084 --> 01:49:17.580
because this is another cool story.

2647
01:49:17.580 --> 01:49:19.330
I didn't know anything about this guy,

2648
01:49:19.330 --> 01:49:22.070
like the foreign service dude.

2649
01:49:22.070 --> 01:49:24.490
Jay Taylor, this is another story.

2650
01:49:24.490 --> 01:49:27.490
Actually, this section of the book came.

2651
01:49:27.490 --> 01:49:30.270
He was also another pen pal of mine

2652
01:49:30.270 --> 01:49:34.170
but he was a pen pal, like many, like a long time ago.

2653
01:49:34.170 --> 01:49:35.450
He had read something that I had written

2654
01:49:35.450 --> 01:49:39.550
for the New York Times about swimming

2655
01:49:39.550 --> 01:49:42.020
as the last refuge from connectivity.

2656
01:49:42.020 --> 01:49:44.730
And he said, you know

2657
01:49:44.730 --> 01:49:47.880
I have a story for you about how it brought people together.

2658
01:49:47.880 --> 01:49:50.611
And it was about community, about connection.

2659
01:49:50.611 --> 01:49:52.760
And he was this foreign service guy

2660
01:49:52.760 --> 01:49:57.160
who got sent to Iraq for a posting for a couple of years.

2661
01:49:57.160 --> 01:49:59.200
I think it was 2008 to 2010.

2662
01:49:59.200 --> 01:50:02.920
And it was a time when Baghdad was seeing

2663
01:50:02.920 --> 01:50:06.580
a lot of like shelling activity.

2664
01:50:06.580 --> 01:50:09.100
And so it was a pretty hairy time to be there.

2665
01:50:09.100 --> 01:50:11.560
And he was in the green zone

2666
01:50:11.560 --> 01:50:16.260
which at the time was part, was in situated in

2667
01:50:16.260 --> 01:50:18.810
Saddam Hussein's Republican palace.

2668
01:50:18.810 --> 01:50:20.840
Now, I don't know if you know this

2669
01:50:20.840 --> 01:50:24.943
but Saddam Hussein and his sons were very fond of swimming.

2670
01:50:26.002 --> 01:50:27.740
And so at all of their palaces

2671
01:50:27.740 --> 01:50:32.740
and homes around Iraq, they had these crazy pools.

2672
01:50:33.690 --> 01:50:37.810
Like just again, like luxury upon luxury in the desert

2673
01:50:37.810 --> 01:50:41.490
is like having a pool that you keep filled.

2674
01:50:41.490 --> 01:50:46.050
And this pool had multilevel diving boards.

2675
01:50:46.050 --> 01:50:48.780
In fact, you can see, there are some,

2676
01:50:48.780 --> 01:50:49.613
I don't know if they're still up

2677
01:50:49.613 --> 01:50:52.941
but there are some YouTube videos of service,

2678
01:50:52.941 --> 01:50:54.080
Soldiers swimming, yeah.

2679
01:50:54.080 --> 01:50:57.240
Soldiers like jumping off of the diving boards,

2680
01:50:57.240 --> 01:50:59.910
which doesn't actually, it doesn't,

2681
01:50:59.910 --> 01:51:03.320
see, watching them doesn't speak well of us.

2682
01:51:03.320 --> 01:51:04.242
I don't know.

2683
01:51:04.242 --> 01:51:05.075
There's just something so-

2684
01:51:05.075 --> 01:51:06.280
Well, we just, we descend upon the palace

2685
01:51:06.280 --> 01:51:08.620
and take it over, and turn it into like a playground.

2686
01:51:08.620 --> 01:51:13.110
It's that kind of like imperialist view

2687
01:51:13.110 --> 01:51:14.430
that has so gotten us into trouble.

2688
01:51:14.430 --> 01:51:16.113
But anyway, so,

2689
01:51:17.530 --> 01:51:19.300
but in this time of war, of course

2690
01:51:19.300 --> 01:51:22.060
there is a community that forms around this pool because

2691
01:51:22.060 --> 01:51:24.040
of course, everyone is drawn to this oasis.

2692
01:51:24.040 --> 01:51:26.250
Yeah who wouldn't wanna be at that pool?

2693
01:51:26.250 --> 01:51:27.660
Yeah and suddenly they're like, wait

2694
01:51:27.660 --> 01:51:28.620
we're allowed to use this pool?

2695
01:51:28.620 --> 01:51:31.427
It is like got fountains and tiles and diving boards.

2696
01:51:31.427 --> 01:51:33.930
And it just is glorious.

2697
01:51:33.930 --> 01:51:37.160
And again, it's peace, it's finding quiet.

2698
01:51:37.160 --> 01:51:38.800
And he said that when he

2699
01:51:40.810 --> 01:51:42.280
would get in this pool

2700
01:51:42.280 --> 01:51:44.450
he would just, then that's when he stopped hearing

2701
01:51:44.450 --> 01:51:46.190
like the firing range.

2702
01:51:46.190 --> 01:51:47.210
Like you know, you'd be muffled.

2703
01:51:47.210 --> 01:51:49.600
And otherwise it's like this incessant noise

2704
01:51:49.600 --> 01:51:51.373
of like military exercises.

2705
01:51:52.270 --> 01:51:57.270
And, you know, there the people who were there

2706
01:51:58.950 --> 01:52:00.600
were from all over the world, you know

2707
01:52:00.600 --> 01:52:04.980
they're diplomats, soldiers, UN peacekeepers, translators,

2708
01:52:04.980 --> 01:52:08.183
local Iraqis, who were, you know, there providing support.

2709
01:52:09.080 --> 01:52:14.080
And he saw that that pool was again

2710
01:52:14.810 --> 01:52:16.800
like everyone, all the animals flocked

2711
01:52:16.800 --> 01:52:17.890
to the watering hole, right?

2712
01:52:17.890 --> 01:52:22.580
But this, in this case it was like a psychological space.

2713
01:52:22.580 --> 01:52:23.760
Like a coping mechanism.

2714
01:52:23.760 --> 01:52:24.690
A coping mechanism.

2715
01:52:24.690 --> 01:52:29.150
And he was swimming.

2716
01:52:29.150 --> 01:52:31.500
He taught swimming lessons for a long time.

2717
01:52:31.500 --> 01:52:33.340
He was a lifeguard, you know

2718
01:52:33.340 --> 01:52:37.123
had grown up in the Baltimore suburbs, I think DC.

2719
01:52:38.189 --> 01:52:42.110
And he has, you know great form

2720
01:52:42.110 --> 01:52:44.569
and he started to teach.

2721
01:52:44.569 --> 01:52:45.980
A lot of people would come up to him, and say

2722
01:52:45.980 --> 01:52:46.813
"Hey, you got any tips for me?"

2723
01:52:46.813 --> 01:52:50.820
And he would see his colleagues flailing around in the pool

2724
01:52:50.820 --> 01:52:51.720
and he would offer some tips.

2725
01:52:51.720 --> 01:52:54.070
And he's a very, like when you read the book,

2726
01:52:54.070 --> 01:52:55.570
you understand that he's just like a very kind,

2727
01:52:55.570 --> 01:52:56.403
he's a teacher.

2728
01:52:57.387 --> 01:53:01.293
His joy and his role in life is to be a teacher.

2729
01:53:04.723 --> 01:53:08.810
And you've encountered many coaches over your life

2730
01:53:08.810 --> 01:53:09.643
as have I.

2731
01:53:09.643 --> 01:53:13.410
And the best coach is one who

2732
01:53:14.330 --> 01:53:16.840
kind of instills a little bit of fear. (laughing)

2733
01:53:16.840 --> 01:53:19.790
But that you want, it's authority.

2734
01:53:19.790 --> 01:53:20.850
You seek that approval though.

2735
01:53:20.850 --> 01:53:22.450
You seek that approval.

2736
01:53:22.450 --> 01:53:24.254
I wouldn't say that he's a person.

2737
01:53:24.254 --> 01:53:26.780
Oh, I don't know what, what he is like as a parent

2738
01:53:26.780 --> 01:53:30.200
but he's a very gentle guy at this point in his life.

2739
01:53:30.200 --> 01:53:32.640
But you see that he has this quiet authority

2740
01:53:32.640 --> 01:53:35.967
that you want to, you know that he knows things

2741
01:53:35.967 --> 01:53:38.610
and you want him to share them with you.

2742
01:53:38.610 --> 01:53:40.110
And so he was able to kind of,

2743
01:53:40.949 --> 01:53:42.400
I mean I think of him as a pied piper

2744
01:53:42.400 --> 01:53:44.014
of all of these swimmers

2745
01:53:44.014 --> 01:53:46.960
in the green zone, because he kind of was.

2746
01:53:46.960 --> 01:53:50.880
He ended up building this, you know, Baghdad swim club

2747
01:53:50.880 --> 01:53:53.750
of like a roster of 250,

2748
01:53:53.750 --> 01:53:57.240
some odd people rotated over two years

2749
01:53:58.540 --> 01:54:00.600
and would be teaching swimming lessons.

2750
01:54:00.600 --> 01:54:03.520
I mean, they kept having to add classes

2751
01:54:03.520 --> 01:54:05.311
because they kept coming to inquire.

2752
01:54:05.311 --> 01:54:06.144
It's like a feel good movie.

2753
01:54:06.144 --> 01:54:07.771
Yeah it's totally a feel good movie.

2754
01:54:07.771 --> 01:54:09.030
Jay Taylor folks.

2755
01:54:09.030 --> 01:54:13.890
You can contact me for the rights with him. (laughing)

2756
01:54:13.890 --> 01:54:17.120
I didn't realize what really struck me

2757
01:54:17.120 --> 01:54:21.823
aside from that story is this discussion around,

2758
01:54:23.800 --> 01:54:25.660
you know how beautiful the sunsets are

2759
01:54:25.660 --> 01:54:27.320
and like how people would swim,

2760
01:54:27.320 --> 01:54:29.160
like sort of at dusk or dawn,

2761
01:54:29.160 --> 01:54:32.100
like not at high noon, it was too hot.

2762
01:54:32.100 --> 01:54:33.800
But when the sand would kick up

2763
01:54:33.800 --> 01:54:36.510
that's when you would get the really epic sunsets.

2764
01:54:36.510 --> 01:54:39.960
But that was also the danger signal

2765
01:54:39.960 --> 01:54:42.730
because that's when the incoming mortars

2766
01:54:42.730 --> 01:54:43.563
would occur, right?

2767
01:54:43.563 --> 01:54:45.660
Because that sand would obscure

2768
01:54:47.350 --> 01:54:50.650
the ability to find the people who'd been hit.

2769
01:54:50.650 --> 01:54:51.483
Right.

2770
01:54:51.483 --> 01:54:55.110
Right, it was cover for,

2771
01:54:55.110 --> 01:54:57.350
it was a good mortaring opportunity.

2772
01:54:57.350 --> 01:55:02.350
And it's just like to exist in that reality.

2773
01:55:02.420 --> 01:55:03.818
Like, you're in this,

2774
01:55:03.818 --> 01:55:04.820
you have this pool with fountains

2775
01:55:04.820 --> 01:55:07.690
but right outside of it, like people are getting shelled.

2776
01:55:07.690 --> 01:55:08.523
Yeah.

2777
01:55:10.210 --> 01:55:11.290
And, you know

2778
01:55:12.570 --> 01:55:16.970
Jay almost got hit by a mortar in his trailer.

2779
01:55:16.970 --> 01:55:19.218
Like on the third day he was in Baghdad.

2780
01:55:19.218 --> 01:55:21.660
He really almost didn't live to

2781
01:55:23.970 --> 01:55:26.330
have this club, you know teach these people to swim.

2782
01:55:26.330 --> 01:55:30.570
And it sort of underlines this

2783
01:55:31.490 --> 01:55:33.860
just how, again porousness between states.

2784
01:55:33.860 --> 01:55:36.600
And that's one of the big themes of the book is like

2785
01:55:36.600 --> 01:55:39.163
where I think with swimming,

2786
01:55:40.090 --> 01:55:42.810
what's so intriguing to me about it is that it is a sport,

2787
01:55:42.810 --> 01:55:43.770
it is a practice.

2788
01:55:43.770 --> 01:55:45.910
It is something we do for exercise,

2789
01:55:45.910 --> 01:55:46.743
but it is,

2790
01:55:48.991 --> 01:55:51.550
it is the difference between life and death and water.

2791
01:55:51.550 --> 01:55:52.820
That is the reality.

2792
01:55:52.820 --> 01:55:54.150
I mean, that is crazy.

2793
01:55:54.150 --> 01:55:57.903
And we don't dance very often.

2794
01:55:58.840 --> 01:56:02.130
It's that close these days to death.

2795
01:56:02.130 --> 01:56:05.510
I mean, last year, this past year

2796
01:56:05.510 --> 01:56:08.560
has been an exceptional year to that.

2797
01:56:08.560 --> 01:56:10.680
But in our modern day we don't experience

2798
01:56:10.680 --> 01:56:14.360
that acute acuteness.

2799
01:56:14.360 --> 01:56:16.370
Most of us don't anyway,

2800
01:56:16.370 --> 01:56:17.910
most of us are lucky not to.

2801
01:56:17.910 --> 01:56:21.820
And so I think sports of course,

2802
01:56:21.820 --> 01:56:24.740
competition is a way to experience

2803
01:56:24.740 --> 01:56:26.403
this acuteness of being, right.

2804
01:56:29.620 --> 01:56:31.073
The adrenaline rush,

2805
01:56:33.820 --> 01:56:37.310
the urgency of feeling just really alive.

2806
01:56:37.310 --> 01:56:39.303
And, and oftentimes that,

2807
01:56:41.440 --> 01:56:43.230
you know, that feeling

2808
01:56:44.440 --> 01:56:47.550
is something that we kind of can only really approximate

2809
01:56:49.140 --> 01:56:51.460
with something that's a very heightened experience.

2810
01:56:51.460 --> 01:56:52.400
Right.

2811
01:56:52.400 --> 01:56:55.300
But swimming allows you to practice

2812
01:56:55.300 --> 01:56:58.600
literally putting yourself in an unnatural environment.

2813
01:56:58.600 --> 01:57:00.073
Yeah.

2814
01:57:00.073 --> 01:57:01.870
One that can really, again,

2815
01:57:01.870 --> 01:57:03.590
certainly, especially with the ocean

2816
01:57:03.590 --> 01:57:08.590
can really rob you of that life in pretty short order

2817
01:57:09.040 --> 01:57:11.060
if you're not careful. (laughing)

2818
01:57:11.060 --> 01:57:12.571
I don't want to scare people.

2819
01:57:12.571 --> 01:57:13.570
No.

2820
01:57:13.570 --> 01:57:14.403
I don't think about that.

2821
01:57:14.403 --> 01:57:15.730
Like I, you know, that doesn't occur.

2822
01:57:15.730 --> 01:57:18.153
I'm so acclimated to swimming, like

2823
01:57:18.153 --> 01:57:19.670
pool, forget about it.

2824
01:57:19.670 --> 01:57:21.950
Like, I don't think about a pool being dangerous at all.

2825
01:57:21.950 --> 01:57:24.160
Like, it's just, that is my natural habitat.

2826
01:57:24.160 --> 01:57:25.690
I'm more hyper aware of that

2827
01:57:25.690 --> 01:57:27.459
when I'm in the ocean, of course.

2828
01:57:27.459 --> 01:57:29.080
Right, but you were talking about

2829
01:57:29.080 --> 01:57:29.913
how you don't like it.

2830
01:57:29.913 --> 01:57:30.746
Yeah I know.

2831
01:57:30.746 --> 01:57:32.370
I mean, that's definitely a thing, but

2832
01:57:32.370 --> 01:57:33.850
but like in the pool, like the idea

2833
01:57:33.850 --> 01:57:36.070
that something bad is gonna happen to me

2834
01:57:36.070 --> 01:57:38.033
is not anything that I think about.

2835
01:57:39.010 --> 01:57:41.080
But that is a very real thing for a lot of people.

2836
01:57:41.080 --> 01:57:41.913
Right.

2837
01:57:41.913 --> 01:57:44.725
I mean, and I think that's super interesting.

2838
01:57:44.725 --> 01:57:46.709
And I wanted this book

2839
01:57:46.709 --> 01:57:51.709
to explore that with that reader.

2840
01:57:52.020 --> 01:57:55.270
I wanted to acknowledge that reality

2841
01:57:55.270 --> 01:57:56.680
that that fear is real.

2842
01:57:56.680 --> 01:57:58.817
Like, so the people who don't like swimming,

2843
01:57:58.817 --> 01:58:03.270
who are afraid of the water, that is very,

2844
01:58:03.270 --> 01:58:04.550
that is a profound fear.

2845
01:58:04.550 --> 01:58:07.650
And that is something that once you get to,

2846
01:58:07.650 --> 01:58:11.760
you know to become an adult, to be a certain age where

2847
01:58:11.760 --> 01:58:14.970
you feel that that door is completely closed to you

2848
01:58:14.970 --> 01:58:17.190
because that fear is so profound

2849
01:58:17.190 --> 01:58:20.100
and it's so primal,

2850
01:58:20.100 --> 01:58:22.410
and it is something that

2851
01:58:22.410 --> 01:58:26.270
you have to be fucking brave to actually push past that

2852
01:58:26.270 --> 01:58:29.400
and say I want to learn how to swim.

2853
01:58:29.400 --> 01:58:32.552
And I've talked to quite a few folks who,

2854
01:58:32.552 --> 01:58:35.820
you know had recently started to take lessons

2855
01:58:35.820 --> 01:58:40.800
or had started to

2856
01:58:40.800 --> 01:58:44.470
try to address their, you know,

2857
01:58:44.470 --> 01:58:47.260
their fears and their lack of swimming ability

2858
01:58:47.260 --> 01:58:49.500
over the course of the last couple of years.

2859
01:58:49.500 --> 01:58:54.500
And it's, you know, it is very much tied to

2860
01:58:54.860 --> 01:58:56.660
like some bad experiences they've had maybe

2861
01:58:56.660 --> 01:58:57.863
when they were kids,

2862
01:58:59.010 --> 01:59:01.090
but also there's just a lot of,

2863
01:59:01.090 --> 01:59:02.904
there's a lot of baggage there.

2864
01:59:02.904 --> 01:59:03.737
Well, you're very vulnerable.

2865
01:59:03.737 --> 01:59:05.097
You're essentially naked, you know

2866
01:59:05.097 --> 01:59:06.910
and you're putting yourself in this environment

2867
01:59:06.910 --> 01:59:08.840
where you can't breathe.

2868
01:59:08.840 --> 01:59:11.680
Your whole life you've like said talk to the hand, you know?

2869
01:59:11.680 --> 01:59:13.800
So it is courageous.

2870
01:59:13.800 --> 01:59:17.570
It's doable, if you submit to the process, you know.

2871
01:59:17.570 --> 01:59:18.840
it is a weird thing.

2872
01:59:18.840 --> 01:59:21.170
It's like violin or, you know, anything else

2873
01:59:21.170 --> 01:59:22.910
like if you don't learn it when you're a young kid

2874
01:59:22.910 --> 01:59:24.400
it's a lot harder when you're older

2875
01:59:24.400 --> 01:59:26.550
but that doesn't mean that you can't do it.

2876
01:59:29.150 --> 01:59:31.430
And I know plenty of people who learned later in life

2877
01:59:31.430 --> 01:59:33.410
and just love it now.

2878
01:59:33.410 --> 01:59:35.510
And it does serve,

2879
01:59:35.510 --> 01:59:37.810
you know, everybody has their own relationship with it.

2880
01:59:37.810 --> 01:59:40.110
I know that your relationship with it has changed over time

2881
01:59:40.110 --> 01:59:40.960
as has mine

2882
01:59:40.960 --> 01:59:44.580
but what will always remain consistent is this,

2883
01:59:44.580 --> 01:59:47.240
you know , flow like experience that you have

2884
01:59:47.240 --> 01:59:50.550
in water that I can't replicate in any other way.

2885
01:59:50.550 --> 01:59:53.240
You cite like Moby Dick in that, right?

2886
01:59:53.240 --> 01:59:55.218
Like this idea of sea dreaming,

2887
01:59:55.218 --> 01:59:58.130
like meditation and water are wedded forever,

2888
01:59:58.130 --> 02:00:00.360
which is a line out of that book.

2889
02:00:00.360 --> 02:00:01.193
Yeah.

2890
02:00:03.194 --> 02:00:04.240
Keeps me going.

2891
02:00:04.240 --> 02:00:05.730
Yeah.

2892
02:00:05.730 --> 02:00:06.690
It is meditation.

2893
02:00:06.690 --> 02:00:07.780
It is and it isn't,

2894
02:00:07.780 --> 02:00:10.490
it's a specific type of meditation.

2895
02:00:10.490 --> 02:00:11.873
It's moving meditation.

2896
02:00:13.980 --> 02:00:17.650
Actually someone had told me recently

2897
02:00:17.650 --> 02:00:21.279
that they stopped doing yoga once they discovered swimming.

2898
02:00:21.279 --> 02:00:22.112
Really?

2899
02:00:22.112 --> 02:00:23.063
Because it did

2900
02:00:23.063 --> 02:00:24.010
for them.
Serve that

2901
02:00:24.010 --> 02:00:25.386
function for them.

2902
02:00:25.386 --> 02:00:26.569
Yeah.
Yeah.

2903
02:00:26.569 --> 02:00:27.470
Yeah I'm not surprised

2904
02:00:27.470 --> 02:00:29.932
but you know what, it's better when you do both.

2905
02:00:29.932 --> 02:00:31.714
They're different, you know?

2906
02:00:31.714 --> 02:00:33.340
They're different.

2907
02:00:33.340 --> 02:00:35.640
And surfing is a whole different thing, right?

2908
02:00:35.640 --> 02:00:37.880
Which is another aspect of swimming,

2909
02:00:37.880 --> 02:00:39.270
I suppose on some level,

2910
02:00:39.270 --> 02:00:42.720
but it's own unique relationship with water

2911
02:00:42.720 --> 02:00:44.513
and symbiotically living

2912
02:00:44.513 --> 02:00:45.710
with the elements.
Yes.

2913
02:00:45.710 --> 02:00:46.543
And timing.

2914
02:00:46.543 --> 02:00:49.440
I mean, I thought about that this morning

2915
02:00:49.440 --> 02:00:52.163
and when I got out was just, you have to,

2916
02:00:53.040 --> 02:00:55.590
it's the same principle of you have to time it right.

2917
02:00:55.590 --> 02:00:57.050
You have to read the water right.

2918
02:00:57.050 --> 02:01:00.370
And then you have to move in just the right way

2919
02:01:00.370 --> 02:01:02.110
to get on the wave.

2920
02:01:02.110 --> 02:01:04.150
Otherwise it's not pretty.

2921
02:01:04.150 --> 02:01:05.590
Yeah.

2922
02:01:05.590 --> 02:01:08.300
So I suppose you're going to swim for the rest of your life.

2923
02:01:08.300 --> 02:01:09.133
I hope so.

2924
02:01:10.213 --> 02:01:12.042
They better open the pools back up soon.

2925
02:01:12.042 --> 02:01:14.550
Yeah, yeah.
Right?

2926
02:01:14.550 --> 02:01:19.550
I know a lot of mer-people out of water these days,

2927
02:01:19.620 --> 02:01:21.280
they just, they're not the same.

2928
02:01:21.280 --> 02:01:25.470
Don't you feel more just,

2929
02:01:25.470 --> 02:01:28.210
I feel like when I get in water in the morning,

2930
02:01:28.210 --> 02:01:29.830
I'm just a better person.

2931
02:01:29.830 --> 02:01:33.300
I just feel like it's all smoothed back.

2932
02:01:33.300 --> 02:01:35.790
But way better, I'm way more capable.

2933
02:01:35.790 --> 02:01:37.120
And when I said they better open the pools soon

2934
02:01:37.120 --> 02:01:40.300
let me please like couch that in context.

2935
02:01:40.300 --> 02:01:41.820
Like, I'm not saying be unsafe.

2936
02:01:41.820 --> 02:01:43.950
I just wish we could be in a situation

2937
02:01:43.950 --> 02:01:46.150
where it was, where pools could safely open.

2938
02:01:47.080 --> 02:01:50.020
Because I know that I'm a better person and I enjoy it.

2939
02:01:50.020 --> 02:01:51.790
And I miss it.

2940
02:01:51.790 --> 02:01:53.494
I know you do too.

2941
02:01:53.494 --> 02:01:56.020
What do you, let's close it with this thought, which is

2942
02:01:56.020 --> 02:01:59.520
what is it that you want people to walk away from

2943
02:01:59.520 --> 02:02:00.703
after reading the book?

2944
02:02:02.530 --> 02:02:06.923
I think it's a sense of possibility, right?

2945
02:02:09.430 --> 02:02:11.900
I just think about water these days.

2946
02:02:11.900 --> 02:02:15.380
And I've given this a lot of thought lately,

2947
02:02:15.380 --> 02:02:18.990
especially because I can't do it.

2948
02:02:18.990 --> 02:02:22.090
I can't do it as readily as I once could

2949
02:02:22.090 --> 02:02:24.993
and I took it for granted too, like anyone else, right.

2950
02:02:26.120 --> 02:02:27.950
That I could get in the water whenever I wanted to

2951
02:02:27.950 --> 02:02:29.533
and find that peace, find that,

2952
02:02:31.436 --> 02:02:32.390
you know, smoothing back of feathers

2953
02:02:32.390 --> 02:02:33.710
and that time with myself.

2954
02:02:33.710 --> 02:02:36.590
And now it's like this juggling act of trying to find

2955
02:02:36.590 --> 02:02:38.730
again like the lane reservation or the window

2956
02:02:38.730 --> 02:02:40.490
where like I know how to manage all,

2957
02:02:40.490 --> 02:02:44.143
you know, stuff at home with my kids and work and whatever.

2958
02:02:45.339 --> 02:02:46.270
And my husband

2959
02:02:46.270 --> 02:02:49.170
who only supports me, by the way, getting out in the water,

2960
02:02:49.170 --> 02:02:50.130
he's just like, go.

2961
02:02:50.130 --> 02:02:50.963
Please go.

2962
02:02:50.963 --> 02:02:53.220
Please go, we'll all be happier.

2963
02:02:53.220 --> 02:02:54.470
Does he swim?

2964
02:02:54.470 --> 02:02:58.010
He is, he's a pretty good swimmer.

2965
02:02:58.010 --> 02:03:00.180
He enjoys the water.

2966
02:03:00.180 --> 02:03:01.090
He hates the cold.

2967
02:03:01.090 --> 02:03:02.170
He's like you.

2968
02:03:02.170 --> 02:03:05.430
He will not get in the cold water.

2969
02:03:05.430 --> 02:03:09.647
I mean, when we woke up the other morning and it was,

2970
02:03:09.647 --> 02:03:11.430
it was like, you know, one of those mornings

2971
02:03:11.430 --> 02:03:12.510
that where you could see your breath.

2972
02:03:12.510 --> 02:03:15.047
And that's not that usual here in California guys,

2973
02:03:15.047 --> 02:03:16.570
as in many places.
This time of year though.

2974
02:03:16.570 --> 02:03:18.820
I mean, it's high 30s at night here

2975
02:03:18.820 --> 02:03:22.478
but then it'll be 82 by like two in the afternoon.

2976
02:03:22.478 --> 02:03:24.100
So he got up and it was before dark

2977
02:03:24.100 --> 02:03:27.360
and I was getting up to go surf

2978
02:03:27.360 --> 02:03:29.793
and he looks at him and he goes, boy,

2979
02:03:30.690 --> 02:03:33.836
what I really wanted to do right now is jump

2980
02:03:33.836 --> 02:03:34.994
in the freezing ocean!

2981
02:03:34.994 --> 02:03:36.920
(both laughing)

2982
02:03:36.920 --> 02:03:39.637
I just had to laugh because I said,

2983
02:03:39.637 --> 02:03:41.650
"You know, that actually, I mean I know it sounds insane,

2984
02:03:41.650 --> 02:03:43.220
but it sounds pretty good to me.

2985
02:03:43.220 --> 02:03:44.610
And that's why I'm leaving.

2986
02:03:44.610 --> 02:03:45.767
I love you, goodbye."

2987
02:03:45.767 --> 02:03:46.847
That's hilarious.

2988
02:03:46.847 --> 02:03:49.240
And so he understands me for sure.

2989
02:03:49.240 --> 02:03:50.980
It took me a long time to come back

2990
02:03:50.980 --> 02:03:52.850
to that kind of appreciation though

2991
02:03:52.850 --> 02:03:56.270
because I was so steeped in competitive swimming for so long

2992
02:03:56.270 --> 02:03:59.750
and the idea of the early morning alarm clock,

2993
02:03:59.750 --> 02:04:02.560
you know and then standing on the freezing cold deck

2994
02:04:02.560 --> 02:04:03.393
in the dark.

2995
02:04:03.393 --> 02:04:06.350
And I was like, I do not need this in my life anymore.

2996
02:04:06.350 --> 02:04:08.420
I did that for many years, you know

2997
02:04:08.420 --> 02:04:10.697
but I've kind of come around to,

2998
02:04:10.697 --> 02:04:12.640
you know, fall in love with it in a new way

2999
02:04:12.640 --> 02:04:14.860
as long as I don't look at the pace clock.

3000
02:04:14.860 --> 02:04:17.190
Cause that will give you PTSD.

3001
02:04:17.190 --> 02:04:18.400
That is going to do it.

3002
02:04:18.400 --> 02:04:20.130
Yeah I think it is interesting.

3003
02:04:20.130 --> 02:04:22.030
And I love to hear you talk about how

3004
02:04:23.009 --> 02:04:26.690
you had to come around full circle from

3005
02:04:28.840 --> 02:04:31.210
what was a very intense

3006
02:04:31.210 --> 02:04:33.030
and formative experience in your life,

3007
02:04:33.030 --> 02:04:35.610
you know, which is to compete at a collegiate level

3008
02:04:36.974 --> 02:04:38.997
in a very competitive school.

3009
02:04:38.997 --> 02:04:41.730
And very gratifying, and one of the highlights of my life,

3010
02:04:41.730 --> 02:04:44.510
but also extremely difficult

3011
02:04:44.510 --> 02:04:47.203
and not without its traumas, you know,

3012
02:04:47.203 --> 02:04:49.060
and like I needed to do other things.

3013
02:04:49.060 --> 02:04:50.550
So there was a long period of time

3014
02:04:50.550 --> 02:04:52.100
where I didn't want anything to do with the swimming pool.

3015
02:04:52.100 --> 02:04:54.890
Yeah, I mean, you have to have enough distance from it.

3016
02:04:54.890 --> 02:04:56.440
And also to understand that

3017
02:04:57.480 --> 02:04:59.900
yeah, like you're at a different stage of your life.

3018
02:04:59.900 --> 02:05:01.810
And I think, you know,

3019
02:05:01.810 --> 02:05:05.670
I like to think about how I came back to swimming as well.

3020
02:05:05.670 --> 02:05:09.070
Like there were times when it wasn't as present in my life

3021
02:05:09.070 --> 02:05:12.020
and now it's kinda come roaring back

3022
02:05:13.250 --> 02:05:15.310
in a very wonderful and real way.

3023
02:05:15.310 --> 02:05:18.580
Do you have a sense that you're more creative

3024
02:05:18.580 --> 02:05:20.660
when you're swimming consistently?

3025
02:05:20.660 --> 02:05:23.433
Like you're more in touch with your creative voice?

3026
02:05:24.660 --> 02:05:25.810
I think so.

3027
02:05:25.810 --> 02:05:29.060
And again, like, it was not something that I thought about

3028
02:05:29.060 --> 02:05:30.960
until I wrote this book.

3029
02:05:30.960 --> 02:05:33.430
Like I did not, you know,

3030
02:05:33.430 --> 02:05:36.200
examine or interrogate why

3031
02:05:36.200 --> 02:05:37.410
I wanted to get in the water

3032
02:05:37.410 --> 02:05:39.310
and then would go and sit and write.

3033
02:05:39.310 --> 02:05:41.159
But unconsciously you were,

3034
02:05:41.159 --> 02:05:43.280
you would not have been compelled to write the book, right?

3035
02:05:43.280 --> 02:05:45.020
Or been interested in exploring that.

3036
02:05:45.020 --> 02:05:47.020
It kinda nudged me that way.

3037
02:05:47.020 --> 02:05:48.460
Yeah?
Yeah.

3038
02:05:48.460 --> 02:05:49.397
Well, thank you.
Thank you.

3039
02:05:49.397 --> 02:05:50.670
For talking to me today.

3040
02:05:50.670 --> 02:05:52.500
I love the book.

3041
02:05:52.500 --> 02:05:53.900
Please go check it out.

3042
02:05:53.900 --> 02:05:55.950
It's called "Why We Swim."

3043
02:05:55.950 --> 02:05:57.983
It's a very direct title.

3044
02:05:58.961 --> 02:06:01.243
It answers that question.

3045
02:06:02.230 --> 02:06:03.120
I really loved it.

3046
02:06:03.120 --> 02:06:06.450
And I'm so happy that the book is being so well received

3047
02:06:06.450 --> 02:06:08.030
and so successful.

3048
02:06:08.030 --> 02:06:09.590
I know you've got another book, right?

3049
02:06:09.590 --> 02:06:14.590
It's like a children's book about a woman, big wave surfer?

3050
02:06:15.100 --> 02:06:18.570
It's "Sarah and the Big Wave."

3051
02:06:18.570 --> 02:06:20.320
And it's about Sarah Gerhardt,

3052
02:06:20.320 --> 02:06:21.760
the first woman who surfed Mavericks.

3053
02:06:21.760 --> 02:06:23.141
Yeah.

3054
02:06:23.141 --> 02:06:25.191
Is she the one that's in "Riding Giants?"

3055
02:06:27.452 --> 02:06:28.970
I don't know.
In the Mavericks segment?

3056
02:06:28.970 --> 02:06:30.254
Oh she probably was.

3057
02:06:30.254 --> 02:06:31.626
There's one woman that's interviewed

3058
02:06:31.626 --> 02:06:35.144
around the Maverick's discussion in that movie.

3059
02:06:35.144 --> 02:06:35.977
I mean it's been so long

3060
02:06:35.977 --> 02:06:37.340
since I've seen that documentary,

3061
02:06:37.340 --> 02:06:38.910
but yeah, it was

3062
02:06:38.910 --> 02:06:41.660
I mean she, 1999 and she's in,

3063
02:06:41.660 --> 02:06:43.170
she lives in Santa Cruz.

3064
02:06:43.170 --> 02:06:45.573
She's a, you know, she's a professor.

3065
02:06:49.261 --> 02:06:51.230
It's a wonderful story.

3066
02:06:51.230 --> 02:06:54.420
And it was my first children's book

3067
02:06:54.420 --> 02:06:56.083
and it was such a joy to write.

3068
02:06:57.070 --> 02:06:58.850
How did you find, like,

3069
02:06:58.850 --> 02:07:01.353
I assume you got an illustrator to work with?

3070
02:07:01.353 --> 02:07:02.186
Mhmm.

3071
02:07:03.330 --> 02:07:05.079
Sophie Diao.

3072
02:07:05.079 --> 02:07:07.370
They asked, my publisher actually asked me

3073
02:07:07.370 --> 02:07:08.840
like they don't normally do it.

3074
02:07:08.840 --> 02:07:11.950
So in publishing, I only just found this out

3075
02:07:11.950 --> 02:07:13.523
when I encountered this.

3076
02:07:14.920 --> 02:07:18.010
Usually, children's books,

3077
02:07:18.010 --> 02:07:19.200
you think that the, you know,

3078
02:07:19.200 --> 02:07:22.750
the writer and the and the illustrator's working together?

3079
02:07:22.750 --> 02:07:23.583
No.

3080
02:07:23.583 --> 02:07:25.690
It's like usually the writer writes the book

3081
02:07:25.690 --> 02:07:27.350
and then they find an illustrator and pair it.

3082
02:07:27.350 --> 02:07:28.183
Really?

3083
02:07:28.183 --> 02:07:29.016
Yeah, right?
No way.

3084
02:07:29.016 --> 02:07:31.300
Cause it's all these books seem so,

3085
02:07:31.300 --> 02:07:32.170
How is that possible?

3086
02:07:32.170 --> 02:07:33.889
Like you have to be sympatico.

3087
02:07:33.889 --> 02:07:35.450
Yeah, exactly.

3088
02:07:35.450 --> 02:07:38.840
And usually the publisher does not consult the author.

3089
02:07:38.840 --> 02:07:39.860
The publisher just chooses.

3090
02:07:39.860 --> 02:07:40.810
But in this case
No way.

3091
02:07:40.810 --> 02:07:42.680
They let me choose.

3092
02:07:42.680 --> 02:07:43.984
And she said, yes.

3093
02:07:43.984 --> 02:07:44.817
They don't consult the author

3094
02:07:44.817 --> 02:07:46.430
and they go and get some illustrator?

3095
02:07:46.430 --> 02:07:47.410
It's two lanes.

3096
02:07:47.410 --> 02:07:50.950
It's like, and they have the author

3097
02:07:50.950 --> 02:07:52.820
and they have illustrator

3098
02:07:52.820 --> 02:07:53.970
and they do the pairing.

3099
02:07:53.970 --> 02:07:56.730
That's like 90% of the time.

3100
02:07:56.730 --> 02:07:57.563
Isn't that fascinating?

3101
02:07:57.563 --> 02:07:58.550
That's shocking to me.
I know.

3102
02:07:58.550 --> 02:07:59.383
cause it seems like you have
It doesn't seem like

3103
02:07:59.383 --> 02:08:01.097
that'd work.
to have it braided together.

3104
02:08:01.097 --> 02:08:02.310
Yeah yeah yeah.

3105
02:08:02.310 --> 02:08:05.500
I mean, I guess it works. (laughing)

3106
02:08:05.500 --> 02:08:09.180
But I feel like they're missing out on an opportunity

3107
02:08:09.180 --> 02:08:10.013
is what I'm saying.

3108
02:08:10.013 --> 02:08:10.846
Yeah, I think so.

3109
02:08:10.846 --> 02:08:14.360
Speaking of which, why didn't you read your audio book?

3110
02:08:14.360 --> 02:08:16.293
Oh, that's a great question.

3111
02:08:18.510 --> 02:08:21.560
I don't know that the option was offered to me

3112
02:08:21.560 --> 02:08:23.240
but they said,

3113
02:08:23.240 --> 02:08:24.100
Should have just said I'm doing it.

3114
02:08:24.100 --> 02:08:26.690
They asked me, well again they asked me who I wanted.

3115
02:08:26.690 --> 02:08:31.690
So they gave me a bunch of different professionals.

3116
02:08:32.080 --> 02:08:35.390
Maybe they just thought they needed a professional.

3117
02:08:35.390 --> 02:08:38.060
But I thought Angie Cane.

3118
02:08:38.060 --> 02:08:39.350
She did a great job.

3119
02:08:39.350 --> 02:08:40.183
But I just think,

3120
02:08:40.183 --> 02:08:42.095
because you're so much a part of the narrative

3121
02:08:42.095 --> 02:08:43.510
that I would've,

3122
02:08:43.510 --> 02:08:45.578
I would've liked to have heard you read it.

3123
02:08:45.578 --> 02:08:46.670
Well thank you, next time.

3124
02:08:46.670 --> 02:08:47.503
Next time.

3125
02:08:47.503 --> 02:08:48.680
Put your foot down!

3126
02:08:48.680 --> 02:08:49.769
It's your book.

3127
02:08:49.769 --> 02:08:51.476
Now I'll just throw my weight around all the time.

3128
02:08:51.476 --> 02:08:52.309
Cool.

3129
02:08:52.309 --> 02:08:54.450
Well, come back and talk to me again some time.

3130
02:08:54.450 --> 02:08:55.754
This was really fun.
I would love to.

3131
02:08:55.754 --> 02:08:56.973
Thank you, Rich.
I appreciate it.

3132
02:08:56.973 --> 02:09:00.010
Where should I direct people who want to learn more

